WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.778
- You know when you get that first sip in?

00:00:01.778 --> 00:00:03.978
Almost clogs your heart, oh yeah.

00:00:03.978 --> 00:00:05.729
- Welcome to Worth It, f**k me.

00:00:05.729 --> 00:00:06.895
- Can one of you clap?

00:00:06.895 --> 00:00:07.728
- Worth It UK.

00:00:07.728 --> 00:00:09.479
The show where we test
three different things

00:00:09.479 --> 00:00:11.409
at three drastically
different price points,

00:00:11.409 --> 00:00:14.830
to determine which is the most worth it.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:15.663
- I'm Rich.

00:00:15.663 --> 00:00:16.496
- I'm Ada.

00:00:16.496 --> 00:00:17.965
- Tea connoisseur.
- Sounds the best.

00:00:17.965 --> 00:00:19.677
- This week, the drink of the UK,

00:00:19.677 --> 00:00:21.405
more important than water.

00:00:21.405 --> 00:00:22.813
- Pretty much guaranteed.
- Tea.

00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:24.805
- Have about 20 cups a day I have to say.

00:00:24.805 --> 00:00:26.214
- Wait is that genuine, like 20?

00:00:26.214 --> 00:00:28.694
- A day without tea means
I haven't left my bed.

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:30.045
It's a bleak day.
- Yeah.

00:00:30.045 --> 00:00:31.560
- Joe!
- Who are you?

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:32.581
- Oh, I'm Joe.

00:00:32.581 --> 00:00:36.576
- Honestly, what about that seat there?

00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:39.397
First stop, Cabman's
Shelter, basically fantastic

00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:41.470
spots to get tea for cab drivers.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:45.236
- You don't have to be a cab driver.

00:00:45.236 --> 00:00:47.407
- They came in around
1800s, a lot of the time

00:00:47.407 --> 00:00:49.826
taxi drivers would take a
break, but go into pubs.

00:00:49.826 --> 00:00:52.351
These things were built
as a sort of rest stop,

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:53.687
that didn't serve alcohol.

00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:55.207
- So they were drunk driving horses?

00:00:55.207 --> 00:00:56.040
- Yeah.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.471
(upbeat jazz music)

00:00:59.471 --> 00:01:02.009
- My name is Tracy Edwards.

00:01:02.009 --> 00:01:04.794
Cabman's Shelter started back in 1875,

00:01:04.794 --> 00:01:07.530
so that's why we have the bar,
for you to tie up the horses.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.290
Anybody can order from the hatch,

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.446
but only taxi drivers
can come and sit inside.

00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:14.918
My press is you just
gotta have boiling water,

00:01:14.918 --> 00:01:18.774
fresh tea bag, and just make
individual tea for everybody.

00:01:18.774 --> 00:01:21.550
I'm very proud of myself,
we should have a cup of tea.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:22.894
- Can I have the Arsenal mug please?

00:01:22.894 --> 00:01:24.183
- That actually belongs to Keith.

00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:25.293
- These are specific mugs

00:01:25.293 --> 00:01:26.277
owned by people?
- Yeah, these are,

00:01:26.277 --> 00:01:29.343
the Werst Ham one is my delivery man.

00:01:29.343 --> 00:01:31.789
So there's some builder at
the window called spider.

00:01:31.789 --> 00:01:33.166
- That's whose everywhere.

00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:36.023
- And you put these crystal
palace ones, so good old spider.

00:01:36.023 --> 00:01:37.317
- Good old spider.
- Yeah.

00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:39.158
- What would happen if
Keith saw me drinking out

00:01:39.158 --> 00:01:40.222
of his Arsenal mug?

00:01:40.222 --> 00:01:41.728
- Don't mess with Keith.

00:01:41.728 --> 00:01:43.942
So today you two are just going to have a

00:01:43.942 --> 00:01:47.350
standard cup of tea, for 70 pence from me.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.262
- Tracy was incredibly
kind and offered us a seat

00:01:50.262 --> 00:01:52.867
in the shelter,
unfortunately our sound man

00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:54.200
broke the rules.

00:01:55.322 --> 00:01:57.981
He's been ejected, disgraced
himself a little bit.

00:01:57.981 --> 00:01:59.581
But you know what, I
kinda like this setting.

00:01:59.581 --> 00:02:01.803
- No, this is a nice Instagram wall.

00:02:01.803 --> 00:02:03.842
- All I need is my horse tied up here.

00:02:03.842 --> 00:02:05.175
- Yeah.
- Cheers.

00:02:07.013 --> 00:02:10.084
- Beautiful cup of standard
English breakfast tea,

00:02:10.084 --> 00:02:11.522
doesn't get better than that.

00:02:11.522 --> 00:02:13.242
- Tracy knows what she's
doing, she's done like

00:02:13.242 --> 00:02:14.659
the perfect mix hasn't she?

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:17.131
- For the first cup of the
day, this is everything I need.

00:02:17.131 --> 00:02:18.554
- There's not really a
science to it, it's more like

00:02:18.554 --> 00:02:21.155
second nature isn't it--
- Yeah, its a feeling for her.

00:02:21.155 --> 00:02:22.378
- If you think about
the amount of tea that

00:02:22.378 --> 00:02:24.258
this shelter has churned out.

00:02:24.258 --> 00:02:26.465
- I think may get my vote.

00:02:26.465 --> 00:02:28.138
- And the price--
- 70p.

00:02:28.138 --> 00:02:29.554
- Anything that has a p after it is a

00:02:29.554 --> 00:02:31.026
bargain these days right?

00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:31.859
- Yeah.

00:02:31.859 --> 00:02:33.714
- Even a bottle of water
is at least a pound.

00:02:33.714 --> 00:02:35.302
- I got a joke.

00:02:35.302 --> 00:02:37.787
Every time I have a
tea, it ends with a pee.

00:02:37.787 --> 00:02:39.514
(laughing)

00:02:39.514 --> 00:02:40.969
- Just go over there mate.

00:02:40.969 --> 00:02:42.891
Do you have a fact?

00:02:42.891 --> 00:02:44.423
- What sort of fact?

00:02:44.423 --> 00:02:46.871
Oh, a tea fact--
- A tea fact.

00:02:46.871 --> 00:02:49.320
- Tea bags were invented in 1908.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:50.153
- No.

00:02:50.153 --> 00:02:51.918
- Yeah, Thomas Sullivan, in America.

00:02:51.918 --> 00:02:52.751
- That's true?

00:02:52.751 --> 00:02:53.584
- Yeah.

00:02:53.584 --> 00:02:54.417
- Okay.

00:02:54.417 --> 00:02:56.984
- Joe where did you get
these facts, Wikipedia?

00:02:56.984 --> 00:02:58.401
- No, see that...

00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:01.944
We are after Timberyard in Soho,

00:03:01.944 --> 00:03:04.879
it prides itself on the
reverse osmosis that

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:06.312
it uses with its water,

00:03:06.312 --> 00:03:07.223
I don't know what that means,

00:03:07.223 --> 00:03:08.895
but it sounds very impressive really.

00:03:08.895 --> 00:03:10.184
- It's interesting that you said water,

00:03:10.184 --> 00:03:12.871
tea is the second most
popular drink in the

00:03:12.871 --> 00:03:14.454
world, after water.

00:03:15.673 --> 00:03:17.242
But I never drink it after water.

00:03:17.242 --> 00:03:19.115
(laughing)

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:21.081
- Joe, get out the cab.

00:03:21.081 --> 00:03:23.932
(upbeat pop music)

00:03:23.932 --> 00:03:26.158
- We're at Timberyard,
in the heart of Soho.

00:03:26.158 --> 00:03:27.575
My name's Darren.

00:03:28.451 --> 00:03:30.571
What we specially add to
tea and coffe, is we wanted

00:03:30.571 --> 00:03:32.157
to make sure it was really high quality,

00:03:32.157 --> 00:03:35.307
we've sold some amazing
teas, the basis of our blend

00:03:35.307 --> 00:03:37.662
is a Kenyan, and then we
have a Rwandan, it's more

00:03:37.662 --> 00:03:40.181
aromatic and it kinda
lifts the Kenyan a little,

00:03:40.181 --> 00:03:43.322
so it balances it off, the
final singular tea in there

00:03:43.322 --> 00:03:46.387
is a Hunan, from China, which
has a natural smokiness,

00:03:46.387 --> 00:03:48.236
with a little bit of hot crust bun like.

00:03:48.236 --> 00:03:49.931
- So blend is like a tea cocktail.

00:03:49.931 --> 00:03:51.587
- We use a reverse osmosis system

00:03:51.587 --> 00:03:54.332
to filter our water,
creates the right balance

00:03:54.332 --> 00:03:57.765
of minerals for a really beautiful tea.

00:03:57.765 --> 00:03:59.927
Service itself is a bit of theater,

00:03:59.927 --> 00:04:02.006
we measure five grams of tea leaves,

00:04:02.006 --> 00:04:05.647
that's brewed with water at 93 degrees,

00:04:05.647 --> 00:04:07.623
its quite specific and then left to sit

00:04:07.623 --> 00:04:10.481
with an additional timer and
we set it for five minutes.

00:04:10.481 --> 00:04:12.207
It seems a long time to a lot of people,

00:04:12.207 --> 00:04:14.583
but this is specialty
tea, it takes a little

00:04:14.583 --> 00:04:16.799
bit longer to brew really well.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:19.227
It's a different process,
it takes you five

00:04:19.227 --> 00:04:21.527
minutes for a really lovely intense cup.

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:24.930
A pot of our English breakfast
tea is three pounds 90.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.526
- Alright, so we have
four and a half minutes.

00:04:27.526 --> 00:04:30.254
- Joe, have you got a joke for us?

00:04:30.254 --> 00:04:32.600
- There are only two main types of tea,

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.120
uppercase and lowercase.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.128
(laughing)

00:04:36.128 --> 00:04:38.961
- That is so bad that it is funny.

00:04:39.975 --> 00:04:41.651
- Oh, do you want to do the honors?

00:04:41.651 --> 00:04:43.899
So this is optimal time to pour the tea.

00:04:43.899 --> 00:04:46.365
Oh look at the color.

00:04:46.365 --> 00:04:49.883
- It's like almost as red as the cup.

00:04:49.883 --> 00:04:50.716
- Cheers.

00:04:52.734 --> 00:04:55.134
- You know, I've discovered,
you're a slurper.

00:04:55.134 --> 00:04:56.166
- No, don't say that.

00:04:56.166 --> 00:04:57.316
What are you talking about?

00:04:57.316 --> 00:04:58.356
- Excuses, excuses.
- I'm not.

00:04:58.356 --> 00:05:00.086
Everyone be quiet.

00:05:00.086 --> 00:05:00.919
- Ooh.

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:01.752
(laughing)

00:05:01.752 --> 00:05:03.501
- Yeah, that, that was accidental.

00:05:03.501 --> 00:05:05.125
I thought the tea was closer to my mouth

00:05:05.125 --> 00:05:08.998
and it wasn't, let me
do that one more time.

00:05:08.998 --> 00:05:10.429
It's hot if you don't slurp.

00:05:10.429 --> 00:05:11.541
It's really hot.

00:05:11.541 --> 00:05:12.509
(laughing)

00:05:12.509 --> 00:05:14.534
Oh, I'm breaking out a sweat.

00:05:14.534 --> 00:05:16.910
- I get what he was
saying about the smell,

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:19.606
this would really pair
well with hot crust buns.

00:05:19.606 --> 00:05:20.798
- With a hot crust bun.
- Yeah.

00:05:20.798 --> 00:05:21.733
- It is lovely actually.

00:05:21.733 --> 00:05:23.241
- It an enhanced standard cup of tea,

00:05:23.241 --> 00:05:24.925
like you could have this in your house.

00:05:24.925 --> 00:05:26.582
- But you'd probably want
to have it with a good show.

00:05:26.582 --> 00:05:28.253
- Something that's on once a week.

00:05:28.253 --> 00:05:29.349
- Do you want the top up?

00:05:29.349 --> 00:05:30.918
A very good size.

00:05:30.918 --> 00:05:32.625
- Like there's some places where the cups

00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:34.288
are almost too big, so
by the time you finish

00:05:34.288 --> 00:05:35.793
your tea its cold.

00:05:35.793 --> 00:05:38.049
- Sometimes when I'm having a
cup of tea, I like to balance

00:05:38.049 --> 00:05:40.945
it because I'm too lazy to reach for it

00:05:40.945 --> 00:05:42.873
and put it on the coffee table.

00:05:42.873 --> 00:05:44.273
- We're going to have
an accident in a minute.

00:05:44.273 --> 00:05:47.001
- I really liked it, I
just feel very content.

00:05:47.001 --> 00:05:49.865
- You want to get your
tea, move off, lets go,

00:05:49.865 --> 00:05:52.793
but here you want a tea,
maybe a brownie, maybe

00:05:52.793 --> 00:05:55.460
a biscuit, a little chit chatty.

00:05:57.361 --> 00:05:59.126
- Royal China Club in Marylebone.

00:05:59.126 --> 00:06:00.188
- Okay, no problem.

00:06:00.188 --> 00:06:02.396
- Next stop, pretty fancy place.

00:06:02.396 --> 00:06:06.104
Prides itself on its extensive
Chinese tea collection.

00:06:06.104 --> 00:06:07.021
- Tea fact.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.392
You know why they call it Macho tea?

00:06:10.392 --> 00:06:11.384
Cause its hard.

00:06:11.384 --> 00:06:12.832
(laughing)

00:06:12.832 --> 00:06:13.704
- Was that a joke?

00:06:13.704 --> 00:06:15.584
- Yes.
- Your facts suck Joe.

00:06:15.584 --> 00:06:18.632
I don't think they call
it Macho, isn't it Macha?

00:06:18.632 --> 00:06:19.799
- It's Matcha.

00:06:22.407 --> 00:06:25.324
(orchestral music)

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:28.388
- I am Jason Chen.

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:30.785
PR manager of the Royal China Group UK.

00:06:30.785 --> 00:06:33.446
We don't add milk to the tea, or sugar.

00:06:33.446 --> 00:06:34.293
We make it plain,

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:36.189
its good for your health, cleansing.

00:06:36.189 --> 00:06:38.133
There are different kinds of tea

00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:41.629
there's the air, the humidity,
the weather, the soil.

00:06:41.629 --> 00:06:44.685
The tea we're tasting today,
are one of the more expensive,

00:06:44.685 --> 00:06:48.926
Da Hong Pao, 50 years old,
the flavor is like the wine,

00:06:48.926 --> 00:06:50.365
vintage tea.

00:06:50.365 --> 00:06:54.048
We can only use special pots,
only brew for time posed,

00:06:54.048 --> 00:06:57.494
otherwise you ruin the clay,
but they're served into

00:06:57.494 --> 00:07:01.061
a different jar, means the
tea leaf, doesn't get too much

00:07:01.061 --> 00:07:04.213
flavor out of it, the
first fill, pour it out,

00:07:04.213 --> 00:07:06.341
clean the extra tea leaves, then on the

00:07:06.341 --> 00:07:08.645
second filling, we start drinking.

00:07:08.645 --> 00:07:11.021
This is such a different
type of tea, I cannot

00:07:11.021 --> 00:07:12.997
find these kind of words in England.

00:07:12.997 --> 00:07:15.238
- Is there anything in
Chinese you would say?

00:07:15.238 --> 00:07:16.071
- Hei-ung Shun.

00:07:16.071 --> 00:07:19.309
Is the feeling, rather then
just a taste on the tongue.

00:07:19.309 --> 00:07:21.286
- So it's an experience.
- Yeah.

00:07:21.286 --> 00:07:24.953
Sell it to the customer
for 80 pounds a pot.

00:07:27.115 --> 00:07:29.165
- The most expensive tea we will probably

00:07:29.165 --> 00:07:30.413
ever taste in our life.

00:07:30.413 --> 00:07:31.533
- Are you gonna shot it?

00:07:31.533 --> 00:07:32.925
- Absolutely not.

00:07:32.925 --> 00:07:34.677
Its all shrouded and mystery.

00:07:34.677 --> 00:07:36.325
I don't really know what
these tea leaves have

00:07:36.325 --> 00:07:38.898
seen exactly where they've come from.

00:07:38.898 --> 00:07:41.367
I love how delicate it is.

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:43.086
- I would buy an incense candle of this

00:07:43.086 --> 00:07:44.269
- I was just thinking that thing,

00:07:44.269 --> 00:07:46.053
that it's exactly like incense.

00:07:46.053 --> 00:07:47.917
You'd want this smell while
you're having a massage.

00:07:47.917 --> 00:07:49.653
We do this, are we doing thing?

00:07:49.653 --> 00:07:50.861
- Yeah.

00:07:50.861 --> 00:07:52.341
- Cheers.

00:07:52.341 --> 00:07:56.133
(suspenseful music)

00:07:56.133 --> 00:07:58.221
- That is a lot lighter then I expected.

00:07:58.221 --> 00:08:00.637
I think its quite earthy.

00:08:00.637 --> 00:08:03.109
- It tastes like this kind of golden, like

00:08:03.109 --> 00:08:07.150
I can't find the right words
to describe the taste of it.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:10.150
The words tea, just tastes like tea.

00:08:11.014 --> 00:08:12.157
- Yeah.

00:08:12.157 --> 00:08:14.949
You know when you have wood
smoked fish and the smell

00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:16.797
you get from that is basically the taste

00:08:16.797 --> 00:08:18.547
that you get in this.

00:08:20.274 --> 00:08:21.232
- Wood.

00:08:21.232 --> 00:08:23.888
- We've said that, another one.

00:08:23.888 --> 00:08:24.785
- Ogre.

00:08:24.785 --> 00:08:26.176
(laughing)

00:08:26.176 --> 00:08:28.064
- What is interesting
is with the other teas

00:08:28.064 --> 00:08:30.056
you take like a pretty big gulp of it,

00:08:30.056 --> 00:08:32.025
this you take a tiny sip.

00:08:32.025 --> 00:08:33.184
- Yeah that's true, I get the feeling,

00:08:33.184 --> 00:08:34.800
the smoothness.
- The throat feeling

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.080
he's talking about.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.024
- It's an experience, its
not about the taste buds,

00:08:39.024 --> 00:08:42.699
its about the feeling you get right here.

00:08:42.699 --> 00:08:44.880
- Andrew, you're the
pro, tell us what flavors

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.797
we are tasting in this.

00:08:51.176 --> 00:08:53.353
- Slurper.
- Slurp in action.

00:08:53.353 --> 00:08:55.443
- Nothing wrong with it.

00:08:55.443 --> 00:08:58.443
(suspenseful music)

00:09:01.328 --> 00:09:03.007
- It really dries out your mouth.

00:09:03.007 --> 00:09:03.936
- It does.

00:09:03.936 --> 00:09:05.416
(laughing)

00:09:05.416 --> 00:09:06.249
Yeah.

00:09:06.249 --> 00:09:07.208
Whose you're worth it winner?

00:09:07.208 --> 00:09:08.519
- Really liked the Royal China Club,

00:09:08.519 --> 00:09:10.447
I thought it was rawer, but Timperyard

00:09:10.447 --> 00:09:12.279
is my winner because its so cozy,

